 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** Evaluating the basis functions is $O(J \cdot K \cdot d)$, where $d$ is the degree. Since $J, K$ are typically small (e.g., 120 segments), this is fast per sample.
--   **Memory Complexity:** Stores $J \times K$ control points and weights. For 120x120 grid, this is negligible compared to the volume data.
--   **Strengths:** Corrects for systematic measurement errors (view angle uncertainty) by learning the coordinate mapping [S1, unofficial].
--   **Limitations:** Requires optimization of control points and weights, adding complexity to the training loop. Sensitive to initialization of weights and control points.
--   **When to use:** When measurement coordinates (angles, positions) are uncertain or calibrated incorrectly.
--   **When not to use:** When measurements are precise and fixed; standard interpolation is simpler.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Evaluation is $O(J \cdot K)$ for the basis function computation and summation, where $J$ and $K$ are the number of segments. This is generally slower than linear interpolation but faster than high-degree polynomial interpolation for large grids.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(J \cdot K)$ for storing control points and weights.
+*   **Strengths:** Differentiable, allowing end-to-end optimization of measurement parameters. Handles uncertainty explicitly.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires optimization of additional parameters (weights, control points). Sensitive to initialization.
+*   **When to use:** When measurement coordinates (view angles, detector positions) are uncertain or inaccurate.
+*   **When not to use:** When measurement coordinates are known precisely and fixed.
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of differentiable uncalibrated imaging, specifically for 2D CT reconstruction with view angle uncertainty [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on NURBS theory [23] and integrates it into a differentiable framework to optimize measurement coordinates alongside the volume [S1, unofficial].
+This method originates from differentiable uncalibrated imaging frameworks, specifically for CT reconstruction where view angles and detector locations are uncertain [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the NURBS-Diff module [S1, unofficial]. The approach allows the network to learn more accurate tilt angles than assumed ones, reducing angle error during optimization [S1, unofficial].
 
